      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "$\\bullet \\textbf{Solution Statement 1}$ Let $n = \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor-1 > \\lfloor x \\rfloor.$ If $x<0,$ then $n <0,$ impossible. If the interval is closed, then $n<\\lfloor 2x \\rfloor <n+1.$ If $n$ is even, i.e $\\lfloor x+0.5 \\rfloor=n, \\lfloor x+1\\rfloor=n+1.$ If $x$ is an integer and $n$ is odd, i.e $\\lfloor x \\rfloor=n-\\frac{1}{2},\\lfloor x+0.5\\rfloor=n.$\n\n$\\bullet \\textbf{Solution Statement 2}$ Today as I put a problem I picked out a few... question mark ( Q) did I notice that there might not be any solution? And why not? (shame on me!) Anyway, it shows that $1/2$ lies between $x$ and $x+1.$ Hence, $\\lfloor 2x \\rfloor-1 < \\lfloor x+1/2 \\rfloor < \\lfloor x+1 \\rfloor$ So, we see that $x+7/4 < 2x < x+3.$ After this, doing the same exact thing that solution 1 did, you should see that $x \\in \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4}).}$\n\nThank you in advance.\n\nAssistant: By the problem, $\\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - 1/2 \\le x < \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor$ so $\\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - 1 \\le \\lfloor x \\rfloor \\le 2x - 1/2$. If $x$ is an integer, this becomes $x \\le 2x - 1/2$, which is $\\lceil x \\rceil \\ge x$. But if $x$ is not an integer, this is $\\lfloor x \\rfloor \\ge x$ by Telescoping or Side-scanning agains the definition of the floor function. This is always true modulo 1 since only one of $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ or $\\lceil x \\rceil$ can be an integer. Therefore, $\\lceil x \\rceil \\ge x > (\\lceil x \\rceil - 1)/2$, or $\\lceil x \\rceil$ odd implies $2x - 1/2 \\ge \\lfloor x \\rfloor$, which negates the above relation. Thus $x$ could be even, forcing $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ odd and vice-versa. Equation simplifies to $0 \\le x \\le 1/2$ - $x + 2 \\le 1$ but we may simplify it to $0 \\le x \\le 1/2 \\implies x \\le 0, 1\\implies x \\le 0.25 \\implies{0 \\le x \\le 0.25}$ The rest is just peeling something up.",
